(project pricklypear-songs
 (doc "Songs: CHORD CHARTS as the front end of a layered music representation — the chapter that makes the later insane features (ultimate-guitar tab, theory analysis, melody lines, MIDI, a DAW, live coding, a browser audio engine) all RENDERINGS instead of rewrites. The thesis: every downstream feature is a pure function of one internal representation, so the only decisions that matter early are the representation invariants — spelled pitches and explicit time. Chart text (what the human types) is high-level source; it parses to a song AST; analysis/transposition/diagrams/MIDI/events are derived at read time and NEVER stored (same discipline as the book: .borg source -> AST -> print/export/verify faces). Companion to borge/music.borg (the audio player owning tracks/playlists/media_blobs) — songs owns NOTATION. The boundary is clean: music plays recorded audio, songs renders what you play; as of the 2026-10-03 plan pass songs ALSO owns COMPOSITION AS PROGRAM (section program-authority): code owns authority, score/performance/sound/manifest are projections of it, rendered audio and video land in media_blobs indistinguishable from recordings, and a renderer matrix (audio|video x offline|live|LLM) hangs off ONE manifest (section rendering).")
 (status partial)
 (* agent note (|2026-10-03 M1 MAKE PASS (branch admin/dev -> system/main): L0 algebra + L1 chart DSL + model + faces (chart/roman/transpose/uke/editor) shipped as libs/songs/lib.pp (104 bindings; vault snapshot in lockstep: pp-sync status songs = 104 both / 0 stale). Kadosh seeded on system/main; verified live: /songs?slug=kadosh, &roman=1 (i iv i Vsus verse / i bVII IV chorus), &key=<k> circle-of-fifths respelling (no double accidentals), &inst=uke (7 diagrams; Esus/A -> Esus4 4400), &edit=1. Authenticated 200, anonymous 303. Kernel gotchas found the hard way: substring is (start LENGTH), mod is truncated (songs/fmod floors), only #f is falsy. M4 (events IR + MIDI) still planned. || 2026-10-03 PLAN-PHASE CODA (roerick + pi; new sections program-authority + rendering): the song is a PROGRAM — code owns authority, score/performance/sound/manifest are projections of it (the workout M3 / vault-def / borge-book shape, fourth application). Decisions: form chart|program directive on chordsong body (a chart is a degenerate constant program; parse(chart(p)) == eval(p) is the honesty test; evaluator = example-eval-class restricted sandbox; seeded deterministic or explicitly :nondet-marked; lazy generative scores pulled window-wise); three-layer IR score/performance/sound with one-way enrichment (MIDI demoted to the LAST sink with a written loss report — the old optional-velocity field was MIDI in costume); renderer matrix modality x tier over ONE manifest (transport + bakes + programs + video {cues sharp, curves dense, program ref}); bake planner = partial evaluation into content-addressed blobs (027 hash-domain synergy; (bake x) is a language directive); quantized hot-swap at loop/bar boundaries for audio AND visual programs alike; engine in ZIG, one source three hosts (browser WASM AudioWorklet / native offline renderer / native live host later) — Oxcaml seriously considered and rejected on the WASM story alone; engine/ lives as a repo subtree; control plane in the image, data plane outside (image never synthesizes, transports, or rasterizes); NOT filmstrip/CDP frames as the video medium (roerick veto, 2026-10-03) — a demoscene sync track derived from composed ground truth instead (the Rocket lineage, done right); visualprog = its OWN row type (visual programs reuse across songs); vidgen = the LLM-video sibling of suno (recipe stored: prompt + seed + section-map; generalize the two into one media-gen package only when duplication bites); full edit-timeline video composition (cuts/layers/camera) deferred to a future sibling package joining the same transport. || 2026-10-03 M4-prime GROUND FLOOR SHIPPED (libs/songs/lib.pp, 123 bindings; vault + system/main in lockstep, body-stale 0): performance + manifest. songs/directives-default (SEEDED data), songs/dir-get, songs/spell-tone, songs/perform-section, songs/perform (score + directives -> performed events; CONTINUOUS fields dyn 0..1 + artic; SPELLED tones per section key), songs/performed-event, songs/manifest-value / manifest-json (JSON wire: form, nondet, seed, performance, bakes[], programs[], video{cues,curves,program}), songs/manifest-response (application/json via bytes-response; a refused/failing program answers {"ok":false,"error":...} on 200 — the /api/eval convention; NOTE the kernel checks is_bytes BEFORE is_status, so a Nopales route cannot return a non-200 WITH a JSON body), and the /songs?fmt=show&slug= face. VERIFIED live (dev image + system/main): parse(chart)==eval(program) true; the PERFORMANCE IR is equal across chart and program front ends (form/nondet legitimately differ); manifest byte-identical across repeated builds; no dotted pairs; native reader round-trips. Found + filed BORGE-1791086509 (effect inference ignores lexical scope: a stray global on a branch poisons forge-effects and the sandbox, which is why the program face refused on admin/personal but not system/main). Engine/bakes/video/MIDI remain the planned tail. || 2026-10-03 OFFLINE ENGINE SHIPPED (engine/ subtree, Zig 0.17): engine/src/main.zig + build.zig, native-only first cut. Manifest JSON on stdin -> 16-bit mono 22050 Hz PCM WAV on stdout; interprets performance events (spelled tone names -> pitch classes -> sines, integer ticks -> seconds via tempo, continuous dyn + envelopes); composes nothing, knows no keys. Unit tests green. VERIFIED end to end on the dev image: scripts/pp-render.sh kadosh --upload --music-track T fetched the real /songs?fmt=show manifest (3 sections / 51 events), rendered a 2.7 MB WAV, pushed it via pp_media as audio/wav, created the music-track row, and /media/<id> served it 200 + Range/206 byte-identical to the engine output; /music lists it with the embedded player. ReleaseFast output byte-identical to debug (deterministic engine). The image never synthesizes (control plane in, data plane out) — the engine is invoked by a laptop-side script, NOT the habitat process. Kernel asks: NONE. || 2026-10-03 PERFORMANCE/SOUND SPLIT DEMONSTRATED (strum + bass register): STRUM is authored in Nopales as performance data — songs/directives-default carries a strum model ((dir . down) (spread_ms . 18)), performed-event passes it through, and the manifest ships it as a JSON object. BASS REGISTER is a SOUND-layer decision the engine makes: the performance layer already emits the slash bass NAME, and engine/pp-render voices it an octave down (C3..B3) with a rounder timbre. The engine also gained a plucked-string timbre (decaying harmonic stack, exponential pluck envelope) and peak normalization. VERIFIED: manifest JSON carries strum {dir, spread_ms} as an object and bass names; the engine render is deterministic (two renders byte-identical); spectral analysis confirms the 110 Hz slash bass appears only in the Esus/A window; v2 audio is a plucked instrument (crest factor 0.16) vs v1's sine wall (0.66). This is the layering working: the engine changed timbre/octave, Nopales changed the emitted performance data, and neither touched the score IR. NOTE the wire idiom: JSON objects come from cons-cell alists ((cons "k" v)), NOT proper 2-lists — the score IR stays dots-free, but MANIFEST-embedded objects use the cons form so (json-emit) writes an object. || 2026-10-03 INBOUND IMPORTER SHIPPED (scripts/pp-transcribe.py): recording -> chordsong chart DRAFT — the inverse of the render engine, and the "importer is a face on the parser" non-goal promoted to built. Laptop-side analysis tier (the suno mirror): habitat never does DSP; script decodes (ffmpeg), then librosa for tempo/beat (with a harmonic-component retry for transient-free pads), Krumhansl key, per-beat chroma chord templates (cosine similarity + a DIATONIC BONUS from the detected key — the L2 songs/fit knowledge run in reverse, which is what fixed G-vs-Gm in Bb — plus a tiered margin so sus/power templates can't co-opt every voicing), and deterministic ~4-bar section blocks; then emits chart text with key/meter/tempo directives. It upserts a chordsong (--save) you correct in /songs?edit=1. VALIDATED against HUMAN GROUND TRUTH: roerick's handwritten Psalm 5 chart (key Bb, Bb Eb / Gm F / Bb Cm, chorus with Bb/C Bb/D slash chords) vs the transcript of the real recording — ALL FIVE true chords found and 92% of the transcript weight lands on them (A# F Gm D# Cm); Kadosh-by-ear ground truth recovered Am/Dm/G/Em/G. ORACLE ROUND-TRIP (the unique self-test this architecture enables): render a chart we wrote with engine/pp-render, transcribe the render back, and recover the same chord vocabulary — proving the IR from both ends against a known source. Emitted drafts validate clean via songs/validate and round-trip through the native reader. Honest limits: sections are fixed blocks (not structural), melody/lyrics not yet extracted (the notation is in the source image; BASIC PITCH/Whisper are the next tier), and tempo on transient-free material falls back (Kadosh pad has no beat to lock). Not promoted as a package (laptop tool, python); chordsong rows it saves are data. || 2026-10-02 chapter created from discussion (roerick + pi), plan phase, no make yet. Worked example and first seed song: Kadosh (Paul Wilbur, Jerusalem Arise; words+E melody Elisheva Shomron), chart transcribed from ultimate-guitar ver-1 tab 1645590 (44 votes, key Am) — the song the design MUST make playable on a ukulele to count as real. Name dropped: 'chard' (a chord chART on a cactus OS) — kept out of the spec; package is the plain word 'songs' (libs/songs, /songs, row type chordsong) so chart-vs-song rows stay unambiguous.|) *)
 (convention nopales-package)

 (section representation
  (doc "The one-way pipeline every feature hangs off: L0 pitch algebra (spelled pitch, key, chord-as-degree-map — pure math) < L1 chart DSL+parser (typed text -> song AST) < L2 analysis (roman numerals, key-fit, transposition — pure reads) < L3 renderings (HTML chart face, transposed view, instrument diagrams) < L4 renderings (score -> performance + sound -> manifest -> renderers across the modality x tier matrix; see section rendering) — and, above the whole stack, the composition PROGRAM (see program-authority: code owns authority, data is projected). TWO INVARIANTS carry all the weight: (1) SPELL PITCHES — the canonical chord form is a spelled root pitch-class plus a degree->semitones map, never a bare integer and never a display string; MIDI numbers are derived; enharmonic spelling follows the KEY'S accidental preference. (2) TIME IS EXPLICIT — every chord event carries exact start/duration in integer ticks (12 per beat: divisible by 2/3/4/6, covers 4/4, 6/8, triplets); 'chords floating over lyrics' is a RENDERING, not a model. (3) WELL-FORMED LISTS — the composition IR uses PROPER LISTS ONLY. Nopales' reader rejects dotted notation by design (lisp/lib/reader.ml), so (cons a b) makes an improper list that can be CONSTRUCTED but never written down or re-read; any model built on (degree . semitones) is therefore unround-trippable. The degree map is a two-element list (degree semitones), and the reader/print pair is the honesty check: (equal? (read-string (str score)) score). Derived forms (roman, transposed, events, MIDI) are memoized reads keyed on content (same pattern as the book parse memo), never rows.")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "a chordsong row round-trips through the native reader with identical score values: (equal? (read-string (str (songs/parse body))) (songs/parse body))")
   (test "a score prints and re-reads identically through the native reader: (equal? (read-string (str score)) score); no dotted-pair value exists anywhere in a score")
   (test "no row of any type persists a roman numeral, a transposition, an event list, or MIDI bytes — derived forms exist only at read time")))

 (section program-authority
  (doc "Songs are PROGRAMS; scores are PROJECTIONS. The same resolution as workout M3 (fluid programs own authority; standardized facts are projected from them), the book (.borg source -> AST -> print/export), and the vault-def pipeline: the recipe is CODE — authored, branched, promoted, content-hashed by the tree machinery you already get for free; the artifact is DATA — derived at read/render time, memoized on content hash, never a row. A chordsong body is SOURCE TEXT with a front-end directive: form chart (the default; the L1 chart DSL — formally a degenerate CONSTANT program) or form program (Nopales source, evaluated through an example-eval-class sandbox: restricted, read-only, budget-capped, same profile as the book faces). HONESTY TEST parse(chart(p)) == eval(p): a chart and the literal program of the same song must yield identical score values; one song, two front ends, one IR. WELL-FORMED-LIST MIGRATION (found in contact with the runtime, admin/dev 2026-10-03): the original chord model used dotted pairs for the degree map ((degree . semitones)). Nopales' reader REJECTS dotted notation, so that form could never be written down or re-read, and the first honesty bridge had to normalize through JSON. DECIDED 2026-10-03 (roerick + pi): the IR is PROPER LISTS ONLY — degrees are the two-element list (degree semitones). The native reader is now the round-trip authority ((read-string (str score)) == score) and songs/score canonicalizes both front ends to the same value decidable with (equal?). JSON remains the WIRE serialization for engines and manifests ((json-emit)/(json-parse)); it is no longer a normalization workaround. The front-end directive is the first non-blank, non-comment line: form chart (default) | form program [:nondet]. DETERMINISM CONTRACT: render twice from one seeded body -> identical manifests and bytes, or the bug is found; every random/groove/humanization source takes an explicit seed; genuinely non-deterministic programs are marked (:nondet) and excluded from identity tests, the reason on record. LAZINESS: generative scores are pulled window-wise (delay/force thunks), never materialized whole; every face and manifest declares its window (finite bars, never the infinite piece). NO static analysis of programs is ever the contract — analysis consumes evaluated score data. Compositions are first-class definitions: (define kadosh ...) in the package vault is exactly as valid a song as a chordsong row — a song can BE a name, and a song-of-programs is just a program calling programs, no reference schema needed.")
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "a literal chart and the equivalent constant program produce identical score values (parse(chart(p)) == eval(p))")
   (test "two renders of one seeded program body produce byte-identical manifests; a :nondet-marked body is excluded from identity checks with the reason recorded")
   (test "an infinite/lazy generative program renders its declared window without materializing the whole")
   (smoke "a runaway program body answers with the book-style (could not run: ...) face, not a hung image")))

 (section algebra
  (doc "L0, pure Nopales, no DB, REPL-testable via pp-local lens. Values are lists (Nopales has no records): a spelled pitch is ((letter alter octave)) with alter in semitones (letter symbol, ints); a pitch-CLASS drops the octave ((e 0) = E-natural). A chord is ((root class) (bass class or ()) (degrees list)) where degrees is an ORDERED list of (degree semitones-above-root) proper 2-lists — e.g. minor 7 flat 5 = ((3 3) (5 6) (7 10)); dotted pairs are never used (invariant 3). A key is ((tonic class) (mode major|minor)) and carries the scale's spelled degrees (natural-minor scale for analysis spelling; harmonic-minor V is the classic borrowed case the analyzer must name). The QUALITY TABLE is the finite morpheme set: (blank)=maj, m, 7, maj7, m7, 6, 9, dim, m7b5, aug, sus2, sus4, add9, dim7 — every suffix maps to ONE degree map; suffixes outside the table are parse errors with the accepted list named (the borge/review lesson: fail fast naming the legal values). Key functions: (songs/parse-chord \"Esus/A\") -> chord value (bass present); (songs/chord-tones chord) -> spelled pitch classes; (songs/pitch-midi pitch) -> int; (songs/key-scale key) -> spelled scale; (songs/transpose-chord chord key-a key-b) -> respelled chord (transposition follows the TARGET key's accidental set — the respelling table is the piece naive apps get wrong: Bb not A# in F); (songs/roman chord key) -> numeral string; (songs/fit chord key) -> diatonic|borrowed|chromatic|pivot.")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "(songs/roman Dm. (key Am)) -> i; (songs/fit A-major-triad Am) matches the C# chromatic test (the pivot case Kadosh actually uses)")
   (test "transpose +3 then -3 on every chord of the Kadosh chart returns the original spelling in the original key")
   (test "every quality-table entry parses and prints back to itself through the chart glyph printer")))

 (section chart-dsl
  (doc "L1 — the chart format, typed in a textarea, line-oriented, forgiving. TIME RULE (iReal lineage): inside a bar group | ... | each space-separated chord glyph consumes ONE WHOLE BAR; a slash splits the bar into equal halves | Am / Esus |; dots or N.C. hold/continue. METER: key/meter/tempo are header directives (key Am, meter 4/4, tempo 70) that set state forward; a section header may override for its span ([Chorus key A]). SECTIONS: [Label] starts a section with inherited context; every bar after it belongs to it until the next header — sections inherit keys DOWNWARD, so modulations are local (the Kadosh chorus shifting Am -> A is the canonical test). LYRICS: any other nonblank line is an annotation carried verbatim in its section, carrying NO time in v1 (lyric-to-beat alignment is a later voice problem, not a chart problem). Comments start with ';'.")
  (details
   (doc (|
WORKED EXAMPLE — the canonical seed chart (Kadosh, UG ver-1 1645590, key Am):

key Am
meter 4/4
tempo 70

[Verse: Kadosh — Hebrew]
| Am  Dm  Am  Esus/A |
Kadosh, kadosh, kadosh.
| Am  Dm  Am  Esus/A |
Kadosh, kadosh, kadosh.
| Am  G  Em  Am  |
Adonai Elohim tz'va'ot,
| Am  G  Em  Am  Esus/A |
Adonai Elohim tz'va'ot.

[Verse: Santo — Spanish]
| Am  Dm  Am  Esus/A |
Santo, santo, santo.
| Am  Dm  Am  Esus/A |
Santo, santo, santo.
| Am  G  Em  Am  Esus/A |
Señor, nuestro Dios de poder —
| Am  G  Em  Am  A |
Señor, nuestro Dios de poder.

[Chorus key A]
| A  G  D  A |
Quien fue y quien es y quien ha de venir.
| A  G  D  A |
Quien fue y quien es y quien ha de venir.
| A  G  D  A |
Asher hayah, v'hoveh v'yavo —
| A  G  D  A |
Asher hayah, v'hoveh v'yavo.

PARSE PRODUCT: a song AST — (((meta (key Am) (meter (4 4)) (tempo 70)))
 (sections (((name ...) (key ...) (bars ((events ...) ...)) (text ...)) ...))).
Each event: ((chord value) (start-tick int) (dur-ticks int)) on the 12-ticks-per-beat grid.
ERRORS: a song parses or returns a list of (line kind message) — never partial ASTs;
unknown quality, malformed meter, lone pipes, and a [section key X] with a bad key all
report with line numbers (drift-as-rendering: the editor face shows them inline, like
book/verify). The PRINTER (songs/chart ast) emits canonical text — the round-trip identity
is the format's honesty check.|))
  )
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "parse of the Kadosh chart yields toggles: verse sections key Am, chorus section key A, 24 bars total, 4 events per line")
   (test "(songs/chart (songs/parse body)) == canonical body for the seed chart (whitespace-bar-level normalization documented)")
   (smoke "an injected typo (Esus/ alone) reports line-anchored errors naming accepted qualities; the row body is never silently coerced")))

 (section model
  (doc "Storage follows the notes-doc pattern exactly. (deftype 'chordsong 'slug 'title 'body 'tags 'day). ONE row per song; body is the chart DSL text and the ONLY authority for key/meter/tempo/sections (no denormalized key field — an out-of-sync label is a drift class we refuse to create; the index face just parses cheaply). Editing is the read-modify-upsert convention from notes: GET the row, POST the new body. Songs are branch-shared data like every data_rows type: your charts on your garden branch, promote to system/main to publish the songbook.")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (smoke "(deftype 'chordsong 'slug 'title 'body 'tags 'day) + upsert of the Kadosh chart on admin/dev, then /songs?slug=kadosh renders it")
   (test "update changing only the body never rewrites title/slug; deleting the row deletes nothing else")))

 (section faces
  (doc "GET-query-driven, zero extra state — every view of a song is a pure function of (row, query): /songs (index: title, parsed key, bars, sections); /songs?slug=kadosh (the chart rendered as a per-section CSS-grid of bar cells with chord glyphs, lyric lines under their bars, key/meter/tempo header); &key=G (TRANSPOSED VIEW — no row mutation, the transpose buttons walk the circle of fifths); &roman=1 (roman numeral under each chord cell + fit coloring: diatonic plain, borrowed amber, chromatic/pivot marked — the analysis ANSWERS the question 'is this chart legal' the way book/verify answers drift); &inst=uke (diagram strip: one small fret grid above the chart for each DISTINCT chord object in the song — the ultimate-guitar feel). Editor: /songs?slug=x&edit=1 textarea POST upserts the body; parse errors list inline under the editor with line numbers. All faces are htmx fragments first (filmstrip pane citizens).")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (smoke "/songs?slug=kadosh renders all three sections with 6 distinct chord cells; &inst=uke shows 7 diagram grids (Esus/A included)")
   (test "&key=C is byte-identical across two GETs an hour apart (a pure read, no writes anywhere)")
   (test "&roman=1 on the Kadosh chart prints i iv VII v (verse) and I bVII IV (chorus, key A) — and flags the Spanish verse's final A as the pivot")))

 (section voicings
  (doc "Chord OBJECTS are instrument-free; VOICINGS are instrument-specific render data. (deftype 'chordsvoicing 'instrument 'chord 'frets 'label 'day) seeds the standard shapes, uke first: frets is 4 comma-joined per-string values (G,C,E,A on uke). The diagram face looks up voicing by (instrument, chord-print) — NOT by display string — so C#m7b5 and Dbm7b5 are the same lookup through the object. THE KADOSH LESSON is the spec case: Esus/A's slash bass is an instrument-relative instruction, unrepresentable on a reentrant uke (no low E, and the C string is the lowest pitch anyway) — the FACE substitutes the voicing Esus4 4400 during rendering; the chart keeps saying Esus/A. Voicing rows seed from libs/songs at package promote: no hand-authorship (vault-authority rule). Left hand of the future: the voice-leading optimizer is explicitly NOT spec'd here (only the storage seam it will read).")
  (details
   (doc (|
SEED UKULELE VOICINGS (standard gCEA, GCEA string order):
Am 2,0,0,0   Dm 2,2,1,0   Em 0,4,3,2   G 0,2,3,2
A 2,1,0,0     D 2,2,2,0     Esus4 4,4,0,0   (Esus/A renders as Esus4)
|))
  )
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (smoke "&inst=uke resolves every distinct chord of Kadosh to a voicing; an unknown chord degrades to a name chip, never a blank cell")
   (test "removing the Esus4 row makes the face substitute nothing silently — a diagram miss is visible (dots render, wrong fingers don't)")))

 (section analysis
  (doc "L2 — the theory face, pure. Given the song AST: per-section key resolution (inherit, override); roman numerals via the L0 algebra; key-fit classification with a four-word vocabulary: diatonic (all chord tones in the section key's scale), borrowed (in the mode's modal-interchange family — natural-minor VII and harmonic-minor V are the common worship pair), chromatic (tones outside both), pivot (a chord containing the NEXT section's key's tonic color — Kadosh's final Spanish-verse A-major-over-Am is the flag case: its C# launches the chorus key). This vocabulary is deliberately small and NAMEABLE — every label must be arguable from first principles (a chord is not 'wrong', it is classified), which is the formalism. Transposition composes with analysis: roman view of a transposed chart is IDENTICAL to the original (key-relative math proves spelling correctness end-to-end).")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "&roman=1&key=G on Kadosh shows the same numerals as &roman=1 — the invariance IS the test")
   (test "fit never returns an untagged error: Esus/A in Am is borrowed+Vsus (harmonic V suspension), G in A-major is bVII borrowed, Dm in Am is diatonic iv")))

 (section rendering
  (doc "L4 — the directed stack EVERY renderer shares: compose -> SCORE (L1 values; spelled pitch, explicit ticks) -> PERFORMANCE (a pure function of score + DIRECTIVES; directives are data — articulation maps, tempo maps, groove, humanization seeds, strum models — and performed events carry CONTINUOUS signal fields: dynamics are 0..1, not velocity; articulation tags; strum models a sink may expand or ignore) and SOUND (patch-as-capability + signal graph: a patch names family/tuning/samples/envelope, NEVER an engine-specific node graph; the chordsvoicing rows generalize into the patch-registry seam at their own rate) -> MANIFEST (the ONE control-plane/data-plane contract every live engine syncs to; just another face: GET /songs?slug=kadosh&fmt=show -> JSON) -> RENDERERS across the modality x tier matrix. RULES: one-way enrichment (a layer enriches, never mutates upstream); every random source seeded; MIDI is ONE lossy projection with a written loss report and ships LAST so its limits can never shape the IR (the old optional-velocity field was the tell that events-as-hub was MIDI in costume).")
  (details
   (doc (|
RENDERER MATRIX — modality x tier, one manifest contract shared across all of it:
  audio:  offline = Zig native render -> media_blob (a render plays through
          /music exactly like a recording; media_blobs is the recorded-vs-
          rendered unification) | live = WASM AudioWorklet in the browser,
          manifest-driven | LLM = suno/ACE-Step (already shipped: libs/suno).
  video:  offline = native rasterizer/frame engine -> media_blob (NOT
          filmstrip/CDP screenshot compositing — rejected 2026-10-03) | live =
          demoscene-style browser WebGL/WebGPU canvas host driven off the
          manifest's video block (the sync track is derived from composed
          ground truth — onsets, section boundaries, chord-color, energy
          curves computed BEFORE any audio exists; the Rocket lineage of
          hand-syncing by ear, made obsolete) | LLM = vidgen, the suno-for-
          video workflow: task -> poll -> media_blob landing, gateway-agnostic
          (config-row base_url, same as suno), RECIPE STORED (prompt + seed +
          section-map; optionally per-section clips cut on bar boundaries via
          the edit/* ffmpeg helpers); suno+vidgen generalize into one media-gen
          package only when the duplication actually bites.
BAKE PLANNER — 'too slow for live' is a feature, not an apology: any
over-budget subtree renders offline into a content-addressed media_blob and
enters the manifest as a bake entry; (bake this-texture) is a composer
directive in the language; bakes memoize on content hash (the 027 hash
domain), so re-renders of unchanged subtrees are free.
HOT-SWAP — live coding commits at quanta: manifests are versioned; a
re-defined composition or visual program swaps in at the next loop/bar
boundary; audio and visual programs swap on the SAME commit.
ENGINE — one Zig engine, three hosts, in-repo subtree engine/: WASM build =
browser AudioWorklet (the browser IS the audio device); native build = the
offline renderer now, a native live host later (projectors, real demoscene
discipline). Oxcaml was seriously considered and rejected on the WASM story
alone — unboxed/stack-allocated OCaml CAN do real-time audio; the browser
target cannot wait for it. SuperCollider shape: Nopales = sclang (control
plane, image + REPL), the engine = scsynth (data plane, outside the image),
the manifest = OSC. The engine composes nothing, analyzes nothing, knows no
keys: it interprets patch programs, plays bakes, runs transport + commits.
VISUAL PROGRAMS — visualprog rows (own type; decided 2026-10-03): program
source, reusable across songs; authored through REPL/edit faces (the
demoscene entry is intrinsically code); executes in the canvas host, reading
the audio clock per frame (audio owns time; video is the guest).
DEFERRED UP: full video composition (cuts/layers/camera/edit timeline) is a
future sibling package with its own IR joining the same transport — same
seam songs/music keep: renderer today, its own language later.
STILL PLANNED FROM 2026-10-02 (chart-side, orthogonal to the matrix):
MELODY m: lines (mono voices in the bar grammar — chord+melody alignment is
free once time is explicit) and TAB as a second INPUT face (string,fret,time
compiling to the same score IR, ultimate-guitar style rendering).
|))
  )
  (status planned)
  (verify
   (test "(songs/manifest body) for the Kadosh chart: chorus entries spell G-naturals (mixolydian borrow survives to the sink), verse Esus/A carries the A-bass; two builds of the seeded body are byte-identical")
   (smoke "engine/pp-render (SHIPPED 2026-10-03): manifest JSON on stdin -> 16-bit mono WAV on stdout; scripts/pp-render.sh kadosh --upload --music-track T brings it back as a media_blob and a music-track row; /media/<id> serves it with Range/206 and the bytes are identical to the engine output")
   (test "a bake directive renders exactly the tagged subtree, the manifest references its blob by content hash, and a re-render of an unchanged subtree is a cache hit")
   (test "MIDI export of Kadosh names every dropped feature (articulation, strum, microtime, dynamics mapping) in its written loss report")
   (smoke "the WASM worklet plays the Kadosh manifest in-browser: bakes play from blobs, the live patch program synthesizes, and re-defining a visualprog swaps the visuals at the next loop point without an audio glitch")
   (smoke "vidgen submits a task, polls to completion, lands a media blob, and the row records its recipe (prompt + seed + section-map)")))

 (section non-goals
  (doc "v1 ships NO: lyric-to-beat time alignment (annotations carry no time — a later voice problem); NO DATA PLANE IN THE HABITAT PROCESS — the image authors and compiles (control plane) but never synthesizes, transports, or rasterizes; engines run outside it (browser asset, native binary); the image never becomes a DAW; NO projections as rows (score/performance/manifests are derived and memoized on content hash, never data_rows); NO filmstrip/CDP frames as a video medium (rejected 2026-10-03); NO MIDI-first anything (MIDI is the LAST sink, a projection labelled with what it drops); NO static-analysis guarantees over composition programs (analysis consumes evaluated scores); NO PDF/print styling beyond browser print; NO voice-leading optimizer (only the voicing storage seam); NO per-user sharing model beyond branches; NO capo emulation (transpose covers it); NO import from ultimate-guitar or ChordPro (though the DSL is deliberately adjacent to both — an importer is a face on the parser, cheap later).")
  (status implemented)
  (verify
   (test "after any songs make, borge drift: no kernel audio-playback substance added — sinks stay file/asset exports")))

 (section sequencing
  (doc "M1 (SHIPPED 2026-10-03, commit 144d156): algebra + parser + chart/roman/transpose/uke/editor faces + model + Kadosh seed — the original M2/M3 folded into the M1 make pass. M4-prime program-authority + the rendering ground floor: form chart|program on chordsong + the sandboxed evaluator; songs/score, songs/perform (+directives), the patch registry generalizing chordsvoicing; the Zig engine/ subtree with NATIVE OFFLINE RENDER FIRST (Kadosh -> media_blob -> plays through /music — a render being indistinguishable from a recording IS the acceptance test). M5 manifest + WASM AudioWorklet host + quantized hot-swap (loop/bar commits; REPL re-definition IS live coding). M6 video tiers: visualprog rows + canvas host + the ground-truth sync track. M7 vidgen (the LLM-video sibling; suno already covers LLM audio) and MIDI export LAST, SMF writer carrying its loss report — it ships only once nothing can be shaped by it. Kernel asks stay additive (binary-safe VStr byte concat + audio/midi whitelist when the MIDI sink lands; the Zig engine needs NOTHING from the image — it never runs in-image). Workstream cards per milestone (borge/workstreams.borg); branches follow the garden ritual: define on admin/dev, promote, snapshot.")
  ; status partial — M1 implemented live; the 2026-10-02 plan extended by the 2026-10-03 program-authority + rendering plan pass.
  (status partial)
  (verify
   (test "borge report after each milestone pass: songs sections flip planned->implemented one at a time; report planned-count decreases monotonically")))
)
